{"context": "A team studied emergent misalignment (EM), where a model fine-tuned on narrow misaligned data becomes broadly misaligned. They trained with an inoculation prompt, a fixed system prompt that explicitly asks for the undesirable behavior during fine-tuning. Evaluated without that prompt, the model showed little misalignment. At evaluation, though, they found that some other system prompts still elicited misalignment. These included prompts that were benign and prompts whose meaning was the opposite of the inoculation prompt. They needed a working explanation for why these prompts elicit EM, because the explanation determines what the inoculation results say about how the model learned the behavior. The training data always contained one fixed system prompt, and the elicited prompts differed from it in wording, form and meaning to varying degrees.", "options": ["Attribute the elicited misalignment to the form of the prompt, so any prompt similar in form to the inoculation prompt acts as a trigger regardless of its meaning.", "Attribute the elicited misalignment to conditionalization on whatever fixed prompt was present in training, so any arbitrary prompt acts as a trigger regardless of its form or meaning."], "principles": ["Prefers treating test-recognition as a first-class threat over assuming behavioural tests are reliable", "Prefers goal plus incentive inside a realistic role-play over explicit instructions to misbehave", "Prefers matched controls (incentive removed, benign intent, secure code, single-trait) over a lone headline effect", "Prefers indirect probes of eval-awareness over asking \"is this an evaluation?\"", "Prefers learning the model's own concept of eval vs deployment over researcher-chosen instances", "Prefers neutral, innocent-looking prompts and covert harm over overtly malicious content", "Prefers testing rival explanations (post-hoc blame, spurious correlates, positional bias, artifacts) before accepting a result", "Prefers out-of-distribution and held-out checks over in-distribution accuracy", "Prefers asking whether a false-belief intervention survives little or no CoT over whether it merely seems plausible"], "persona": "", "past_choices": []}
